WEBVTT

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.760
Hello, my name is Hannah Vogel.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:10.000
I'm an archaeologist and my PhD research
is on disability in history and
archaeology.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.520
I am the curator of this exhibition
titled Disability Heritage: Archaeology
with Difference.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.080
This exhibition is both an online and
physical museum display.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:27.130
The display is at the Gale History Museum
and Macquarie University, Sydney,

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:32.040
Australia from the 10th of November 2026
until the 1st of March 2027.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.160
The online exhibition will be around for
a lot longer than that.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.729
In this video I'll tell you more about
the exhibition I have curated and some of

00:00:41.729 --> 00:00:46.280
the accessibility features we have
incorporated to the display and website.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.880
The introductory panel reads:
Disability Heritage: Archaeology with Difference.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.320
Disability is part of our history.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.520
However, archaeology is often ableist,
overlooking, or worse,

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.000
devaluing disabled experiences.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.280
This display invites you to think
differently.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.840
If we place disability at the centre of
our story,

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.840
the object on the top shelf can represent
the use of mobility aids,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.772
despite not usually being understood as
disability representation.

00:01:19.772 --> 00:01:23.560
The middle shelf challenges the ideal of
a bodily norm.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.078
It features a diversity of bodies,
both human and divine,

00:01:28.078 --> 00:01:32.160
demonstrating differences across cultures
and throughout time.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.320
The feeding vessels on the bottom shelf
are evidence of community and care.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.327
These objects,
created thousands of years apart,

00:01:41.327 --> 00:01:46.080
are products of differences in bodily
experiences within a community.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.800
This display is not only about disability,
it is for the disability community.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.172
The accessibility ‘features’ are disability
heritage just as much as the

00:01:57.172 --> 00:02:01.600
archaeological material behind the glass
in the museum display.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:07.099
The wall panel includes a tactile
fabrication of the scene on the first

00:02:07.099 --> 00:02:10.578
object,
3D prints of the objects on the second and

00:02:10.578 --> 00:02:16.160
third shelves,
a QR code and an iPad to watch this video
or access the website.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:23.160
We also have a nearby Braille and large-print
guide of the panel text and labels
for our website.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.668
Alongside this video we have interactive
3D models, customisable text size,

00:02:28.668 --> 00:02:33.472
audio-visual descriptions and high-resolution
photographs with alternative text

00:02:33.472 --> 00:02:39.119
descriptions which you can zoom in or
explore the artefacts from multiple
perspectives.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:42.840
In the display itself,
there are three shelves.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.440
The top shelf has one object, a pelike.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.760
This pelike depicts the use of a mobility
aid, a staff or cane.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.200
In ancient Greece, staff like objects,
were also symbols of power,
authority and justice.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:04.050
Archaeological narratives are selective
about how these artefacts are interpreted,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:09.200
often rejecting the possibility of
disability coinciding with symbols of
power.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.200
On the middle shelf there are seven small
figurines or amulets.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.800
Figurines were made for various reasons
and were often accessible to many people.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.914
These differently shaped bodies are
votive offerings or amulets used for

00:03:24.914 --> 00:03:27.884
protection,
ensuring health and/or fertility,

00:03:27.884 --> 00:03:30.920
invoking the divine,
or for afterlife beliefs.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:37.400
These are an important reflection of the
reality of diversity, rather than an ‘ideal’
body.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.680
The bottom shelf has three objects.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.067
These feeding vessels,
despite differences in design over time,

00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:48.440
were made to support the needs of people
they represent.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:51.840
Difference creating change in material
culture.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.480
This project has included many members of
the disability community throughout all
stages of its development.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.240
Acknowledgments are featured on the
website.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.108
This exhibition is funded by the National
Disability Research Partnership’s

00:04:06.108 --> 00:04:10.503
inaugural Disability Research Leadership
Program and is part of my PhD project

00:04:10.503 --> 00:04:15.440
supported by the Commonwealth through an
Australian Government Research Training
Program.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.965
If you want to learn more about my
research or other projects,

00:04:19.965 --> 00:04:23.040
you can find my profiles on the website
about page.
